<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">South Carolina</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">South Carolina is a region of the south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/savannah">Savannah</a> River and North Carolina. It was home to Mississippian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiefdom">chiefdoms</a> such as Cofitachequi and saw a short-lived French fort at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlesfort">Charlesfort</a> in 1562. English settlers founded Charles Town in 1670, creating a plantation colony worked by enslaved Africans. In 1860 it was the first state to secede, triggering the formation of the Confederacy.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/south-carolina.jpg" alt="South Carolina" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Design by South Carolina General Assembly, SVG by Steve Hall · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_South_Carolina.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>South Carolina borders North Carolina to the north and northeast, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-ocean">Atlantic Ocean</a> to the southeast, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> to the west and south across the Savannah River. From east to west it has three natural regions: the Atlantic coastal plain, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piedmont">Piedmont</a> and, in the northwestern corner, the Blue Ridge Mountains. The coastal plain makes up two-thirds of the state, and its eastern edge is the Sea Islands, a chain of tidal and barrier islands. It has many salt marshes and estuaries, and natural ports such as Georgetown and Charleston.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>There is evidence of human activity in the area from about 50,000 years ago. When Europeans arrived, around 1500, there were many separate Native American polities, among them the powerful Cofitachequi. About a dozen tribes of Siouan background lived up the rivers of the coastal plain, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cherokee">Cherokee</a> further west and the Catawba along the Catawba River. They lived in villages and relied on agriculture.</p><p>The Spanish were the first Europeans in the area. On 8 October 1526 they founded San Miguel de Gualdape near present-day Georgetown, the first European settlement in what is now the contiguous <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. It began with five hundred settlers and was abandoned eight months later by one hundred and fifty survivors. In 1540 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hernando-de-soto">Hernando de Soto</a> explored the region and the main town of Cofitachequi.</p><p>The state is named for King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-i-of-england">Charles I of England</a>, who first formed the English colony, Carolus being <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> for Charles. The Province of South Carolina was formed in 1712 and became a royal colony in 1719. More than 200 battles and skirmishes of the Revolutionary War were fought within it, and it ratified the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitution-of-the-united-states">United States Constitution</a> on 23 May 1788, the eighth state to do so. As a slave state it was the first to vote for secession, in 1860, and it was readmitted to the Union in 1868. The civil rights movement of the mid-twentieth century helped end segregation and legal discrimination in the state.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>South Carolina is composed of 46 counties. Its capital is Columbia and its most populous city is Charleston. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gullah">Gullah</a> community lives in the state, and the Catawba Indian Nation is its one federally recognised tribe.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “South Carolina” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Cherokee, in their own language Tsalagi, lived in towns across the southern Appalachians, in parts of present-day Georgia, Tennessee, North Carolina, <b>South Carolina</b> and Alabama.</blockquote><a href="/empire/cherokee-nation">Cherokee Nation · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Abraham Lincoln's election in November 1860 brought declarations of secession from seven slave states of the Deep South whose cotton economies depended on enslaved labour: <b>South Carolina</b> on 20 December 1860, unanimously, then Mississippi, Florida, Alabama, Georgia, Louisiana and Texas by 1 February 1861, none of them unanimous.</blockquote><a href="/empire/confederate-states-of-america">Confederate States of America · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1970 the black nationalist activist Walter King moved his followers from Harlem to Sheldon, <b>South Carolina</b>, where they founded the Yoruba Village of Oyotunji.</blockquote><a href="/religion/santeria">Santería · Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>The chroniclers of Hernando de Soto’s expedition describe chiefs borne in litters by their leading men, a woman ruler at Cofitachequi in present-day <b>South Carolina</b>, and the paramount chiefdom of Coosa in north-western Georgia, whose ruler commanded a string of chiefdoms from eastern Tennessee into Alabama.</blockquote><a href="/culture/mississippian-culture">Mississippian culture · Society</a></li><li><blockquote>The first black Baptist churches were founded in Virginia, <b>South Carolina</b> and Georgia before the American Revolution.</blockquote><a href="/movement/first-great-awakening">First Great Awakening · Outcomes</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1526 the enslaved Africans of the Spanish colony of San Miguel de Gualdape in <b>South Carolina</b> revolted, and historians suggest they escaped to Shakori Indigenous communities.</blockquote><a href="/movement/underground-railroad">Underground Railroad · Background</a></li></ul></section>
